  "slug": "motor-plasticity-21b2",
  "summary": "Recent human preprints test whether EEG aperiodic slope, TMS-EEG responses and slow waves index cortical excitation-inhibition balance non-invasively.",
  "synthesis": "Four recent preprints address whether non-invasive measures of cortical excitation-inhibition balance can be read out and manipulated in humans. The dominant thread is the EEG and MEG aperiodic 1/f slope as a proxy for this balance. Closed-loop neurofeedback volitionally modulated the sensorimotor slope, with steeper states associated with reduced motor cortex TMS responses. In children aged 10-18, the MEG exponent tracked parietal-occipital GABA+ levels in typically developing participants but not in autism. Two stimulation studies extend this framing: 1 Hz transcranial temporal interference increased NREM slow-wave density, and prolonged intermittent theta burst stimulation altered left frontal theta responses in a sham-controlled crossover TMS-EEG design. The new element is the joint use of aperiodic readouts, deep-target stimulation and TMS-EEG within one excitation-inhibition framework.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
